  "missing_data": [
    "the lug-contact and ledge compliance — they set s (FEA or bench; lug geometry placeholder, HW.8)",
    "the span: lock_groove_z is a placeholder (02_02 §4.4, HW.8)",
    "a Ti–PEEK temperature gradient (sun on the radome, shaded flange)",
    "PEEK relaxation in the heat and its ratcheting over seasons (52's f is temperature-free)",
    "the ring's force below room temperature and resin swell (88)"
  ],
  "verdict": "Temperature moves the clamp by s N/K, and the lug carries (L0 + s·ΔT_c)/f + s·ΔT_h. At the window top the ratified member wall (0.28 mm) is the s = 0 answer: every N/K of loop sensitivity adds 0.069 mm of wall over the capsule requirement (60 K down, 65 K up) and 0.057 mm over the models' edges, so the member holds only for a loop that does not feel temperature at all. The print floor carries the window floor up to s = 1.72 N/K and the nominal up to 0.75 (requirement); at the placeholder span 1.7 mm that asks a loop no stiffer than 26.4 … 11.5 N/µm, against 298 N/µm for the PEEK column under the lugs alone — 11–26× softer. If the PEEK were the loop's only compliance (s = 19–55 N/K), the window top would need a wall of 1.6–4.1 mm. The lug contacts and the ledge are in series and soften the loop; how much — and so whether the bayonet needs a compliant element, or a thicker wall, or a ring that relaxes — is not computed here (the lug geometry is a placeholder)."
}
